    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d386">

                <pb facs="0577" n="291" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg291"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/2894</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-10-01T18:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 1, 1916, 6 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received October 2, 9.05 p.
                        m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4412. The general attitude of the press together with confidential information
                    received leads me to believe that an early resumption of <pb facs="0578" n="292"
                        xml:id="pg292"/> indiscriminate submarine warfare is not unlikely. It is
                    doubtful whether the Chancellor can continue to withstand the steadily
                    increasing public sentiment in favor of such a step, in view of his increased
                    weakness in the Reichstag. This would presumably take the form of a desperate
                    attempt to stop American supplies and ammunition from reaching the Allies.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
